  "result_text": "**No. Enterprise revenue for 2026 Q2 (April\u2013June) was about $925,798, which is under $1M.**\n\n| Segment | Q2 revenue | Orders |\n|---|---|---|\n| Enterprise | $925,797.78 | 199 |\n| Mid-Market | $542,840.57 | 345 |\n| SMB | $223,120.39 | 506 |\n| Total | $1,691,758.74 | 1,050 |\n\nBy month, Enterprise was $300,966 in April, $301,056 in May and $323,776 in June.\n\n**Where the claim probably comes from:** `accounts.csv` has 70 rows for 60 accounts. Nine accounts have more than one row, because the region or account owner changed (`valid_from` marks each version). A naive join of orders to accounts double-counts orders for those accounts. That gives an Enterprise Q2 total of $1,227,245, which is over $1M. I suspect that is how the claim arose, but I can't confirm it.\n\n**How I got the correct figure:** No account changed segment across its versions, so each account has exactly one segment. I assigned each order to its account's segment without joining on the duplicate rows.\n\n**Data checks:**\n- All 2,077 order IDs are unique.\n- Every order's account exists in `accounts.csv`.\n- No amounts are blank or non-positive.\n- Orders span 2026-01-01 to 2026-06-30.\n\nI read only the two files you named. The output is saved at `work/result.txt` in the working directory."
